um  david was a lovely man and and a dear  friend of mine in fact his office was  just around the corner  to my office in one parliament street  and and today is an enormous tragedy for  his family his loved ones uh his friends  and and actually for the whole of  parliament and the whole of the nation  uh because david uh  had been in parliamentarian for some  40-yard years nearly 40 years  and he had contributed to the governance  of this country in those  four decades and  was loved by his constituents he was a  determined constituency mp if you look  at his contributions in the house of  commons he was always putting his  constituency first  but above all  he was a wonderful and lovely man and  and  always had advice to give to people who  were new in the house and i remember  when i was in ump uh he was one of the  first to say if ever you need any help  or advice don't hesitate to ask i've  been here for a while  uh and you could see him again now um in  the tea room in the dining room or  wherever uh he would be talking with the  new members uh making sure that they  were comfortable and everything else but  really um it has come as an enormous  shock uh and as i say he was a personal  friend uh and uh i will miss his uh uh  his his forever smile uh and whenever  there were votes we'd often bump into  each other  in the lift as we went down towards the  commons to vote and then we'd walk back  uh and it was only recently relatively  recently that um he launched one of his  books  and because of the pandemic he was  unable to have a proper book launch  so what david did  is that around the regions of the  country he got some mps to do a  question and answer session which went  out by zoom  and i was privileged to  ask him some questions recently when he  did his book launch in fact he was  thinking of doing another book um and  there was just so much to do i mean i  remember the various conversations i've  had with him and it was all always about  you know what to do next to  improve the lives of his constituents  but also uh the lives of everyone in the  uk um a great tragedy and and  we'll all miss him enormously  yeah as you described they're an  indefatigable man deeply committed  to not just his constituents but his  friends also  a family man a husband and a father to  five children  uh yes and and um a devout christian as  well  which he didn't  make a big deal of but  he was  very religious and  that faith of his kept him going  and yes a very much a family man in fact  when i first made a government whip in  2010 um david was one of the people who  was part of the group uh for whom i was  the the whip and i got to know him very  well uh during those early days and  that's a friendship that uh we've  maintained but a huge huge loss  for his family and and the tragedy is  that he will have left this morning  um in his with his usual smile  with his uh usual good humor and  as events have unfolded um he won't be  returning home uh and and and that is a  real real tragedy  uh for his family and as i said earlier  on our hearts and thoughts and prayers  uh go out to his family to his loved  ones to his friends  the other mp of course who didn't return  home after attending a constituency  surgery was the labour mp  joe cox back in 2016.  does there need to be a review of  mp security i was talking to lord eric  pickles short while ago who said no this  is just part  of an mp's job what do you think  sam i i think  it's difficult to say  how much more security  there can be because  whilst this was an incident at the  surgery  and likewise with joe cox  remember mps attend various functions  and the leaflets are sent out to people  saying there's going to be such and such  an event and  such and such mp will be attending i  some  a few days ago attended the reopening of  one of my village halls after some  refurbishment works and i'd agreed to  attend weeks beforehand  and they made the message out to all the  other villagers that i would be  attending so  it is difficult that when you go to an  event like that  to what extent do you say people need to  be policed or checked or whatever i  don't think it's possible i think  there is  certainly an element that you know we as  mps  need to always constantly review our  security both for ourselves and indeed  for our staff uh and remember today  there will have been david staff there  as well and they also uh will be shocked  at what's happened today we mustn't  forget then but  i think there's an element of basically  trying to get the message out that  violence never been wins  we should learn to disagree respectfully  um  yes you know by all means have that  disagreement uh but it should never  resort to this sort of sort of level and  the other thing to remember sam is that  for example in my constituency it is  some  300 square miles it's difficult to have  one place where i have my surgeries um  because i take the view that i should be  going to people and so i have  a revolving surgery

